venus (Android): hand the AHardwareBuffers behind guest-exported memory to the app's compositor

The guest gets only the dma-buf fd of exported memory, and its Vulkan WSI passes that fd on to the X server (DRI3)
as a frame buffer. Android's EGL cannot import a bare dma-buf, and reading the buffer through a CPU mapping is slow
(gralloc maps these buffers uncached: about 7 ms for a 1080p frame), which made the frame upload the most expensive
step of presenting. The compositor needs the AHardwareBuffer itself: with it, it reads the frame from a GL buffer
object that shares the memory (GL_EXT_external_buffer), a copy inside the GPU.

For memory the guest driver itself asks to export (its swapchain buffers; ordinary host-visible allocations are not
announced), the render server now sends the buffer over the unix socket named by PMBOS_AHB_SOCKET
(AHardwareBuffer_sendHandleToUnixSocket, preceded by a header with the dma-buf's device and inode by which the
X server finds it again), and a "gone" message when the memory is freed. Without the variable nothing changes.

Also initializes fake_gbm_bo::base: on an allocation failure vkr_gbm_bo_destroy released an uninitialized pointer.
(PMB OS)

--- a/src/venus/vkr_device_memory.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_device_memory.c
